  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nHeat advisory will be in effect for the core heating hours both \ntoday and tomorrow all the way up to Campbell, Union, Knox, and \nSevier counties. Upper ridge is bearing its full might down upon us \nthis week, so everyone needs to think cool thoughts. Thursday is the \nmore likely of the remaining two days of the week to hit daily \nrecords, with both peak heat expected today and the records \nthemselves being a touch lower. Yesterday Chattanooga tied a daily \nrecord and TYS and TRI stopped just short. Today 850 mb temperatures \nwill be reaching their 21C (near seasonal climatological maximum) \npeaks for today into Saturday, and the combination sinking will \nmaximize the heat. \n\nDaily Record High Maximum Temperatures\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge                     \n09-03   100(1954)      98(2011)       96(2011)       99(2011)       \n09-04   102(1954)      101(1954)      99(1954)       100(1954)      \n\nDewpoints have been dipping into the upper 60s at Chattanooga, \nMcGhee Tyson, and Tri-Cities the last couple of afternoons during \npeak boundary layer mixing. These values are a few degrees lower \nthan the low 70s the NBM and other consensus aids want to paint, \nwhich isn't surprising given the NBM's high dewpoint biases. \nRegardless, it will be HOT today, tomorrow, Saturday, and then not \nas hot Sunday into next week.\n\nNot as hot because we have a northeastern trough helping to bring a \n\"cold\" front through on Saturday, and a weak impulse on Sunday. \nFriday looks more isolated coverage, but should a storm grow tall \nenough the thermodynamics are favorable for downburst gusty winds. \nSaturday ahead of the \"cold\" front the RRFS has slightly better \ncoverage, but still on the low end side for coverage and again \ndownburst winds would be the expected hazard. Sunday the NBM is the \nmost bullish on PoPs wise as a combination of the slightly lesser \nupper heights and what the GFS and Euro both depict as a weak \nimpulse pivots through our region, likely helping to initiate \nadditional thunderstorms. Again, main hazards would be gusty winds \nand lightning.\n\nLooking ahead temperature wise, the GFS/Euro seemed to trade dance \npartners while the EPS is still steadfast on a return of the ridge \nmidweek next week. We'll see. Hopefully we get more upper trough \nthan upper ridge. Either way it looks likely Chattanooga will at \nleast be sporting a continued streak of 90+ weather. Water breaks \nfor those with fall sports and outdoors jobs remains of utmost \nimportance for heat safety for the foreseeable future.",
